WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
- Communication is one
of the most important

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.770
feedback topics in consulting.

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:08.370
If you work for one of the
leading consulting firms

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:11.000
and you get a feedback
conversation with your manager,

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.890
it is pretty much clear
that how you communicate

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:15.920
will be one of the key topics

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.740
discussed in this conversation.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:18.573
Hi guys.

00:00:18.573 --> 00:00:19.406
My name is Heinrich,

00:00:19.406 --> 00:00:21.930
and welcome to my channel Firm Learning.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.410
Thank you for watching again.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:24.590
Communication is something

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.950
that pretty much all consultancies cover

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:29.660
in their onboarding training programs.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.230
Young consultants need to take

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.510
dedicated communication classes,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.270
and communication is one of the things

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:39.270
that is discussed most frequently
in feedback conversations

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.260
between young consultants and managers.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.360
When I was a young consultant at McKinsey

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.700
it was pretty common that
after I was on a call,

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.520
after I participated in a client meeting

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.210
and maybe said something there,

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.270
I would get some feedback
from my project lead

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.580
on how I said certain things.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.330
So, of course what you
say is important as well.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:01.770
And if you said something
that might not be considered

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.030
very intelligent or appropriate to say

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:05.230
then yes of course,

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.730
this would be addressed
in the feedback sessions.

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:10.820
But at least as likely
you will get feedback

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.810
on how you said certain things.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:15.810
So, even though maybe what you said

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.690
from a content perspective
made absolute sense,

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:22.640
the way how you phrased it, how
you structured your message,

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.820
this is something that
as a young consultant

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.570
you will get tons of feedback in in most

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.020
of the large strategy consulting firms.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.460
And the reason I believe is quite obvious

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.020
because in many ways it is not always

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:36.420
just about what you say,

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.630
but also how you convey your message.

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:43.340
To what extent you are
able to actually persuade

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:46.210
the other person that
what you say is correct.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:48.350
To what extent you help the other person

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:52.210
to actually remember the
message points that you have,

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.430
and then be able to also to tell these

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:57.460
two or three key arguments to other people

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:01.810
in an organization in an
intuitive and persuading way.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:03.910
So, what consultancies commonly do

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:07.660
to train the junior consultants
in these training programs

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.060
to do role plays, and these role plays

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.850
frequently go something like this, right?

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.530
You are being asked to
imagine yourself sitting

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.140
in the team room somewhere at the client,

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:20.420
and now the client walks in,
and maybe even a board member,

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:21.550
or another senior leader

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:24.527
of the client organization
walks in and asks you,

00:02:24.527 --> 00:02:26.127
"Hey Heinrich, how is it going?

00:02:26.127 --> 00:02:27.580
"What is the status," right?

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.360
And often you're taken a bit off guard,

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.322
maybe you're in the middle
of doing something else,

00:02:32.322 --> 00:02:34.280
and now the senior leader
or this board member

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.980
walks into your team room
and asks you to quickly

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.200
and to the point summarize
what exactly you're doing

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.820
and what the status is.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.750
And as maybe you can imagine
this puts many people

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.100
into quite a high amount of stress,

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.570
and many people struggle to
in a precise and concise way

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:54.890
and actually summarize the
key results of the project

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:57.270
and give a concise and professional answer

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.020
to this question of the board member.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.840
And in this video I will
bring up one of the points

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.290
that is really frequently
addressed in feedback sessions

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:07.290
after these role play situations.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.450
And also in situations in real projects

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:13.080
where maybe indeed a senior
member of the client walks in,

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.670
and you then as a young
consultant say something

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:17.150
which may not have met the standards

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.680
of professional and concise communication.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.670
One of the key points that
is brought up in these

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:25.450
kind of feedback sessions
is the distinction

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:30.340
between process and content
when you communicate.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:32.200
When you answer such a question,

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.210
when you're asked to give a status report,

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:36.530
or asked to report on any project matter,

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:38.170
or any other business matter,

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:39.740
if you look at it in a stylized way

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:42.140
there are two ways how
you can talk about it.

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:45.210
It's either focused on the process

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:47.180
or it's focused on the content,

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.540
and let me explain to you
what exactly I mean by that.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.820
So, let's start with process,

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.660
what does it mean if you talk process?

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.030
So, let's imagine that you've
been working on a project

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:58.790
that has been about sizing

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:01.280
market opportunities
in different countries.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.510
So, your client or maybe
your team lead asked you

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:05.800
to analyze different geographies

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.110
and understand if there is a country

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:10.530
where it might make sense for your company

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.200
to enter the market with a certain product

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.050
that you as a compony are offering.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:18.910
So, if your communication
focuses a lot on process,

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:22.650
this means that you focus a
lot on the different steps

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.300
that you took to get to a certain result.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.440
So, you talk a lot about
what you actually did

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.280
in the last days, in the last
weeks, in the last months.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:33.180
So, an example could be
something like this to say well,

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:36.340
I talked to the colleague
in the finance department

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:38.530
and then I got some numbers there.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:40.350
And then I put some market reports

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:42.500
to actually combine the data,

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.320
and then I also looked into
macroeconomic information

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:46.960
about the different countries.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:48.890
And then I combined these things,

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:50.070
but then I realized that actually

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.410
I'm missing certain information.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:55.180
So, I went to the
marketing department head,

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.740
and then I talked to her to understand

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:59.970
her opinion about these geographies.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:01.377
And then again I compiled it,

00:05:01.377 --> 00:05:03.530
and then we had some alignment meetings

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:05.800
with other people of the organization.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.420
And then on looking at all of this

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.650
this is the result that
we came up with, right?

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:15.210
So, this is a process-oriented
way of communicating,

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:18.650
and it focuses a lot on
the things that you did

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:21.460
in order to get to a certain result.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.600
And many people like to
talk about the process.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.040
This is something that many people

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.040
somehow naturally tend to,

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:31.920
to talk about the process.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:35.770
And probably due to the fact
that it really underlines

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:39.220
what all the effort is that
you put into something.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:43.210
It underlines that you
really spent lots of time

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:44.470
on a certain task.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:47.790
It underlines that you
really worked a lot,

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:50.520
that there were just many,
many things that you did,

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:53.170
and you really made a big
and significant effort

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:54.300
to solve this problem.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.960
But I hope that you also see the downsides

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.040
of talking about things in this way

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:02.690
because the focus is on
the steps that you took,

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:05.460
but the focus is not on results.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:08.440
And now try to put yourself
into the shoes of a CEO

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:10.810
of a large blue-chip company,

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:14.130
and you can assume that
he assumes that usually

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:16.400
the people he works with are professionals

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.670
and that they will make
sure that the things

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:21.010
that they present to
him meet a certain level

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.510
of quality and of analytical rigor.

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:26.530
And the truth is the CEO of such a company

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:30.510
just will not care about all
the meetings that you had,

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:33.110
all the time that you put into something,

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:35.303
all of the analysis that you took

00:06:35.303 --> 00:06:37.520
and then maybe found out that
this actually didn't work

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:40.010
so that then you needed to
look into something else,

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:41.890
and this then maybe worked or not.

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:44.203
The CEO will not care, okay?

00:06:44.203 --> 00:06:47.270
He does not care about all
the sweat, all the work,

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:49.420
all the hours you put into something.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:51.870
What he cares about is the result,

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:54.600
and if you talk so much about the process

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.210
you will just waste his time.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:57.043
And this is something

00:06:57.043 --> 00:06:59.600
you should be very cautious
about to not waste the time

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:02.590
of your coworkers,
especially if you operate

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:04.270
in such a professional environment.

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:06.630
So, this is now the process-oriented way

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:08.220
of talking about something.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:11.720
Let's now look into the
content-oriented way.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:14.270
How would you phrase an
answer to the question

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:16.410
of what you did on a project in a way

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:19.310
that actually focuses
much more on the content

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:21.170
but not so much on the process?

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:23.210
And talking about content basically means

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:25.140
talking about the results.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:26.440
So, let me give you an example.

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:29.770
What would that mean
to talk about results?

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:31.210
So, if you talk about the content

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:33.640
you could tell your CEO something like,

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:36.310
so from the analysis that
we did so far we conclude

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:39.470
that France likely is
the country where for us

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:43.430
it would make most sense to
enter with this new product

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:45.760
that we have, and for three reasons.

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:48.720
First, consumer research
shows that this product

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:51.210
really meets the needs of French consumers

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:53.160
in a way that it is not the same

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:54.740
with the consumer behavior patterns

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:56.230
that we see in other countries.

00:07:56.230 --> 00:07:58.400
The second reason is that
the competitive situation

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.330
in these countries is that currently

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:02.830
there is no strong competitor there

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:04.350
offering a similar product.

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:06.920
So, if we go there now
we have an advantage

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:08.210
because we are the first,

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:10.610
and we can now quickly
grow in market share

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:13.250
before maybe others then
join the market later.

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:15.280
The third reason is that our operations

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:17.900
are very well set up to
enter the French market

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:19.780
because we have warehouses,

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:22.940
we have a supply chain set
up that is very effective

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:25.460
in serving the French
market at the moment.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:27.930
And it's much better suited
for the French market

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:29.460
than compared to other markets

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:30.820
which might be served instead.

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:31.910
So, now let's make a cut here.

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:34.180
This is just an example
of how you could phrase

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:37.210
such an answer that now much
more focuses on the content.

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:39.960
Of course now the arguments
that I said were arbitrary

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:42.520
and depend on the specific
situation that you're in,

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:43.670
but I hope that you see the difference

00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:47.050
that what I did not do is explaining

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:48.610
what all the steps were,

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:50.820
what all the data is that I analyzed,

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:53.070
what the meetings were that I had.

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:56.260
What maybe first I thought,
but then I needed to revise

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:58.010
because I found out that
it doesn't make sense

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:00.510
and something else actually
makes much more sense.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:02.997
All of the things I did not talk about,

00:09:02.997 --> 00:09:06.000
but what I did talk about
is what the result is

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.970
and what the key arguments are

00:09:07.970 --> 00:09:10.530
that actually back up this result.

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:12.213
And now ask yourself
what would be the answer

00:09:12.213 --> 00:09:15.960
that the CEO likely would
be much more interested in?

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:17.620
And from my experience in working

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:19.360
for several years in consulting

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:21.530
I can tell you that the
latter is the proper answer

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:22.850
to give in these situations.

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:24.240
The latter answer is an answer

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:25.490
that somebody who communicates

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:27.510
professionally and effectively will give.

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:30.500
So, talking about the content
is really the way to go,

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:32.240
I cannot stress this enough.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:35.860
Communication is so crucial in
any professional environment,

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:38.900
and focusing on content instead of process

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.000
when talking about things that you did,

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:42.460
when giving status updates,

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:43.850
when talking about your projects,

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:44.730
when talking about the things

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:47.000
that you're working on is really crucial

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:50.200
and distinguishes you as a
professional communicator.

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:53.710
And of course now I talked a
lot about consulting examples,

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:55.790
I talked a lot about
the consulting context,

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:59.250
but the truth is that this
type of communication behavior

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:02.460
is relevant to pretty much
any job that you have.

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:03.900
It really does not depend at all

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:05.600
in which industry you're in,

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:07.160
making sure that you focus

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:10.210
on professional communication
is absolutely key.

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:11.140
And what you'll notice,

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:13.890
even when you work in
large blue-chip companies,

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.310
that a lot of people really struggle

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:18.720
with good and effective communication.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:21.360
Even senior leaders of
some of these companies

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:23.320
really struggle with
effective communication.

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:25.580
This is something where
you can actually improve

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:27.380
very quickly and make a difference,

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:30.140
and just shine in contrast
to all these other people.

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:32.320
And the key phrase that just summarizes

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:34.430
this kind of communication pattern,

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:36.240
so if you're looking for
a name for all of this

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:41.100
to do further research it's
called top-down communication.

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:44.250
Top-down communication is the holy grail

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:46.150
in professional communication.

00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:47.650
And what I just talked about,

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:50.220
the distinction between
process and content,

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:51.960
is just one aspect of it.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:55.040
There are many other aspects
in top-down communication.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:55.923
So, I'd like to hear from you.

00:10:55.923 --> 00:10:57.850
Are you interested to learn more

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:00.290
about what professional
communication is all about?

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:02.650
Let me know and I might
cover other aspects

00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:05.630
of this type of communication
in future videos.

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:07.270
And just as a quick tip if you just want

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:09.010
to get started right away,

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:12.170
the Bible of this top-down
communication approach

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:13.577
is a book that is called

00:11:13.577 --> 00:11:16.550
"The Pyramid Principle" by Barbara Minto.

00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:18.240
It's a book that at least at McKinsey

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:20.440
almost every consultant reads,

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:22.690
and some training programs
you even get it as a gift

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:24.940
as something you're supposed
to read in order to advance.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:27.360
And I could highly communicate this book.

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:30.420
I will leave a link to this
book in the description below,

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:32.070
so if you're interested just check it out.

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:34.190
So, let me know if you would
